<article><header><p class="eyebrow">Depression and mood / Emotional wellbeing</p><h1>Feeling like a burden: understanding the thought and finding support</h1><p class="lead">Feeling that other people would be better off without you can be painful and isolating. The thought can feel convincing, but it is not a measure of your worth and it is not something you have to manage alone.</p><div class="blog-answer"><strong>In short:</strong> Perceived burdensomeness is a term used in suicide research for believing that one's existence or needs burden others. Research finds an association with suicidal thoughts, but it cannot predict an individual's actions. If this thought is yours, tell someone you trust and seek support; if you may act on thoughts of suicide, get urgent help now.</div><p class="editorial-note">General information, not a risk assessment or substitute for care.</p></header>
<nav class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><ol><li><a href="#meaning">What the term means</a></li><li><a href="#research">What research can and cannot tell us</a></li><li><a href="#respond">Ways to respond to the thought</a></li><li><a href="#support">When and how to seek support</a></li><li><a href="#urgent">Urgent support in India</a></li><li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li></ol></nav>
<section id="meaning"><h2>What does perceived burdensomeness mean?</h2><p>In research, perceived burdensomeness describes a person's belief that they are a burden to people they care about. It is a painful perception, not proof that others see them that way. Illness, financial strain, caregiving responsibilities, conflict, isolation and many other circumstances can shape how someone feels; there is no single cause.</p><p>Some theories of suicide include perceived burdensomeness alongside other factors, such as feeling disconnected. These are research frameworks, not diagnostic tests or explanations that fit every person.</p></section>
<section id="research"><h2>What research can and cannot tell us</h2><p>A meta-analysis found that perceived burdensomeness was associated with suicidal ideation across the studies reviewed. An association in groups does not tell us what one person will do, establish cause, or replace a direct conversation about safety. A person's circumstances and risk can change, so take statements about being a burden seriously and respond with care.</p><p>The U.S. National Institute of Mental Health lists talking about being a burden among possible suicide warning signs. Warning signs are reasons to check in and connect someone with help; they are not a way to predict a particular outcome.</p></section>
<section id="respond"><h2>Ways to respond to the thought</h2><p>When the thought appears, try naming it as a thought: “I am noticing the belief that I am a burden.” This may create a little room to decide what to do next; it does not prove the thought false or replace professional support.</p><ul><li>Tell one person plainly that you are having a hard time and what kind of support would help right now.</li><li>Choose one manageable next step, such as sitting with someone, arranging an appointment or asking for help with a specific task.</li><li>If you are supporting another person, listen without arguing about whether they “should” feel this way. Ask directly whether they are thinking about suicide and help connect them to support.</li></ul><p>You do not need to solve the feeling before asking for company or help.</p></section>
<section id="support"><h2>When and how to seek support</h2><p>Consider contacting a mental-health professional if the thought persists, affects daily life, or comes with hopelessness, withdrawal, intense distress or thoughts of self-harm. You can begin with a doctor, psychiatrist, psychologist or counsellor. An assessment can explore what is contributing and what support may fit your situation.</p><p>If you are in Greater Noida, you can read about <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatric consultations</a>, <a href="/therapy">therapy</a>, or <a href="/book">requesting an appointment</a>. A webpage or appointment request is not emergency support.</p></section>
<section id="urgent"><h2>Urgent support in India</h2><p>If you might act on thoughts of suicide, cannot stay safe, or someone is in immediate danger, call <a href="tel:112">112</a> or go to the nearest emergency department. You can also call the Government of India's Tele-MANAS helpline at <a href="tel:14416">14416</a> or <a href="tel:1800-89-14416">1800-89-14416</a>. Details are available on the <a href="https://dghs.mohfw.gov.in/national-mental-health-programme.php" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Directorate General of Health Services website</a>.</p></section>
<section id="sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ol><li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5730496/">The Interpersonal Theory of Suicide: a systematic review and meta-analysis</a> (2018).</li><li><a href="https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/warning-signs-of-suicide">National Institute of Mental Health: Warning Signs of Suicide</a>.</li><li><a href="https://dghs.mohfw.gov.in/national-mental-health-programme.php">Government of India, Directorate General of Health Services: Tele-MANAS</a>.</li></ol><p>This article does not diagnose or predict suicide risk. No named clinician review is claimed.</p></section></article>
